{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>You shall not add to the word, etc.</b> Granted, 'you shall not add' is well said, for one might have supposed that there is the principle of two hundred against one hundred. But why do I need 'you shall not subtract'? For one who subtracts even one of all the commandments of HaShem is already commanded and standing under a prohibition not to subtract even one of all the commandments. And in our composition עוֹלְלוֹת אֶפְרַיִם, discourse on this portion, we explained that 'you shall not subtract' is not a commandment but rather an explanation — like that which the Sages, of blessed memory, said (Berakhot 29a): לֹא תִרְוֵי וְלֹא תֶחֱטָא, 'do not get drunk and do not sin' — for 'do not sin' is not a commandment, since that is self-evident; rather, its meaning is: do not get drunk, so that you do not sin. So too, the meaning of 'you shall not add' is: and then, consequently, 'you shall not subtract' — for whoever does this falls through that, since anyone who adds subtracts. And this is like giving a reason for 'you shall not add,' and there we elaborated. And perhaps this matter was joined here because it resembles the sin of Moses, for he added in the striking of the rock, and through addition there came a diminution in faith. Therefore it says: ״וְעַתָּה יִשְׂרָאֵל שְׁמַע אֶל הַחֻקִּים״ — what is 'and now'? Was it not before this incumbent upon them to heed the statutes? Rather, 'and now' refers to the conclusion of the decree that he mentioned before this, and the narrative continues on to the statement 'you shall not add.'" "And there are those who say that the statement 'you shall not add' is connected to the matter of Peor, for every pious fool supposes that a commandment demands that he violate it, and transgresses 'you shall not add,' and in the end they come to diminution, for they bowed down to their gods. And it refers to what is stated: ״וַנֵּשֶׁב בַּגָּיְא מוּל בֵּית פְּעוֹר״, and concerning this he said, as though bringing proof of this, and said: ״עֵינֵיכֶם הָרֹאוֹת אֵת כָּל אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה ה׳ לְבַעַל פְּעוֹר״ — regarding the one who worships Peor, for he is a ba'al, a master, of that form of worship."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {0 "Resolved שפ״ה as an abbreviation for שַׁפָּה, indicating 'this portion'; resolved רז״ל as חֲכָמִים זִכְרוֹנָם לִבְרָכָה, 'the Sages, of blessed memory.' The Aramaic phrases מַאן דְּעָבֵיד הָא נָפִיל בְּהָא and מָאתַיִם מָנֶה are rendered literally, preserving the Talmudic idiom and the halakhic principle of מָאתַיִם מָנֶה (a quantitative argument of two-hundred-vs.-one-hundred) respectively.", 1 "The wordplay בַּעַל as 'master/husband' and as the proper name of the deity Ba'al Peor is preserved by rendering 'for he is a ba'al of that form of worship.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:11:17.915769603Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}